            "- **Strengths:** The model demonstrates strong performance in predicting 'Low' flood severity, as indicated by high precision (1.00), recall (1.00), and f1-score (1.00). This is likely due to the large number of 'Low' severity instances in the dataset, which the model learned effectively.\n",
            "- **Weaknesses:** The model struggles significantly with predicting 'Medium' and 'High' flood severity.\n",
            "  - For 'High' severity, the precision, recall, and f1-score are all 0.00. This means the model did not correctly identify any 'High' severity events in the test set, and any predictions it made for 'High' were incorrect. This is likely a direct result of the very small number of 'High' severity instances in the dataset (imbalance).\n",
            "  - For 'Medium' severity, the precision is 1.00, but the recall is only 0.20, resulting in a low f1-score of 0.33. This indicates that while the model's predictions for 'Medium' are often correct when it makes them (high precision), it misses a large proportion of the actual 'Medium' severity events (low recall). This is also likely influenced by the data imbalance.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Implications for Flood Prediction and Management:\n",
            "- The model is currently reliable only for predicting low-severity flood events. While this can be useful for general awareness and minor preparedness, it is insufficient for managing and responding to more impactful floods.\n",
            "- The inability to predict 'Medium' and especially 'High' severity events effectively is a significant limitation for disaster management, where accurate warnings for severe events are critical for saving lives and minimizing damage.\n",
            "- Predicting 'Low' severity well might lead to complacency regarding the model's overall effectiveness if only accuracy is considered, masking its poor performance on critical high-impact events.\n",
